{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Oregon is set to end its experiment with decriminalizing drugs, as a new law will take effect, making it a crime to possess small amounts of hard drugs. This law gives individuals caught with illicit drugs the option to either face charges or undergo treatment, which includes completing a behavioral health program. Those who possess drugs for personal use will face a misdemeanor charge, punishable by up to six months in jail.\n\n*** \nThe new law aims to crack down on public drug use and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near certain locations. It encourages counties to create treatment alternatives, allowing them to develop programs tailored to their resources. However, opponents argue that this approach may lead to inconsistent policies across different areas.\n\n*** \nTo be eligible for treatment instead of charges, individuals must meet specific criteria, including no other charges, warrants, or violent behavior, and be medically stable. If they meet these requirements, officers will contact a deflection dispatch line to determine eligibility and connect them with a mobile behavioral health team. This team must make contact within 30 minutes, or the individual will be taken to jail.\n\n*** \nThe new law has sparked debate about its potential impact on those struggling with addiction. P.P.S As people of all age groups are affected by this issue, it is a concern that spans the entire country. When individuals struggle with addiction, they may neglect to eat or prioritize their health, instead focusing on obtaining their next fix, often relying on others while out shopping. The struggle with addiction can be so intense that it feels like it is burning their tongue, a constant reminder of their situation.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00